(a) Every pawnbroker shall make a daily written report to the police department, in such form as may be prescribed by the chief of police, of all property pledged to or brought to him during the twenty-four (24) hours ending at 8:00 p.m. on the date of the report. Such reports shall be typewritten. In addition to any other information required by the chief of police, report shall show:
(1) Name and address of the pawnbroker;
(2) Time of transaction;
(3) Serial number of pawn tickets;
(4) Amount paid or advanced;
(5) Full description or articles, including kind, style, material, color, design, kind and number of stone in jewelry and all identifying names, marks and numbers;
(6) description of persons selling or pawning that includes names, address, color, weight and height; and
(7) Any article which customarily bears a serial number, (i.e., firearms, electronic, etc.) which has had serial number removed of obscured, shall be reported immediately to the police department.
Insufficient reports shall be rejected any pawnbroker shall be deemed guilty of an offense. In addition, pawnbrokers shall make daily reports of all typewriters, adding machines and radios that come into or leave their places of business. Daily report shall be in such form shall contain such information as may be required by the chief of police, but shall contain sufficient information to identify each article.
(b) In addition, each pawnbroker shall obtain from each person pawning any article(s), the fingerprint of the right hand index finger, unless such finger is missing (in which event the print of the next finger in existence on the right hand of the person pawning the article(s)) and notation shall be made as the exact finger printed. All prints shall be made on forms to be furnished by the Port Wentworth police department and the pawnbroker shall obtain all other information called for on said form furnished. Fingerprints and the information as required herein shall be obtained from all persons each time such persons pawn any article with such pawnbroker and have been fingerprinted.
(c) The provisions of subsection (b) need not be complied with where the pawnbroker personally knows the person pawning the article(s) or can identify within ninety (90) days after the transaction the person pawning the article(s).
(Ord. No. 92-4, 2-13-92)

